     1	# Chapter 24 — Under the Stone
     2	
     3	The ferryman would not take silver for his rope.
     4	
     5	He said so on the western landing, with the scow rocking at his back and the far drum already groaning. He was the same long sour man who liked animals and nobody else, and he had stood with his arms folded through the whole of Brom's account: the wash, and the well, and a peeled log on a hill of plates, and at the last a gorge with a cart in it. Brom told it as he would have told a debt, in order, leaving nothing out.
     6	
     7	"So it's under a mountain," said the ferryman.
     8	
