books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-11.md:21:There was a dog in the rut, forty meters out from the floor, between them and the stone, because there was always a dog on the road and it was never the one you were looking at. What he had found in the dark was the lane that was not cut — the cord-line, grass and low scrub, south of the rut and parallel to it, meeting the road again at the stone.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-11.md:87:A hundred and forty.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-11.md:151:A hundred and twenty meters of rut, loud the whole way, so that nothing behind him looked south. Sixty. Forty.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-08.md:5:He counted it before he read it. The river was lower than it had been on the sixtieth, and the ford's poles stood a hand farther out of it; the rope ferry was on the near bank, loading; the smith was working, which mattered, because six marks of what was in the satchel belonged to him. The upper inn had smoke. The lower inn had none. The meadow: forty tents and carts where there had been thirty-one, the grass gone to seed and yellow at the edges, the summer's fires fewer and smaller, and of the thirty-one he could find six. The ash was there. The carter with two mules was not — a woman with a hand-cart of hides had the place — and Brom looked at that, and at the ten paces on, where nothing was, and walked to the ten paces on and put the boxes down, one, two, three, with the right arm doing its share for the first time since the ford. The healer had said a week; Brom had given it seven days to the hour, and picked up the middle box with both hands on the eighth morning, and said nothing about it, and nobody else had either.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-08.md:21:Six escorts. Two fence-lines. A wash recovery; a load out of a bog. Four hold contracts on good square paper with brands burned in the corner, each with HOLD in Pike's hand and a name written beside the word. Beside two of the four the name was Teague. Beside the other two — a fence-line at a hold on the east road whose brand was an axe-head, forty marks; a stretch of the north road to be walked ahead of a hold's carts before the autumn, fifty and fed — the name was not a name. It was five names, in a row, spelled as they had been said, in the same hand and the same size as the word beside them.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-08.md:25:"Holds came to the ferry," Pike said, to the page. He had not looked up. "Asked who. I said you." The pen went on. "Axe-head's the fence you cleared cheap the first week. They've a longer one now and they asked by the names, because you've not given me a crew's name and I'll not make one up."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-08.md:29:"Fence a week ago. Road, four days." He finished his line. "They'll wait a fortnight or they'll take Teague. A hold doesn't mind which. That's what a hold is."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-08.md:31:Cael put it where he kept things. Two slips. Forty and fifty. HOLD, and their names. Not a line on a wall — a hold, at the ferry, asking a one-armed man who, and the man saying five names. There was nothing on the shelf beside it that was the same shape.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-08.md:157:Six tables. The carters at the two by the road door, five of them, loud, and the hide-woman with them. The middle table: nobody. The best table by the fire: four chairs pushed in and no jug — Teague's crew out on the east road since dawn on the axe-head hold's other slip, the long fence, HOLD, Teague. Pike behind the book at the table by the fire, with a bowl, and the stone on the book. The sheet on the long wall on its four pegs. And the five of them at the table by the road door, because Brom could see both doors from it, with a jug and a loaf, and Karis's book open, and his own satchel flat on his knee.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-08.md:171:"Teague's nine up," Pike said, to the book, asked by nobody. Then, still to the book, at the same size, as if it were the next line and not a different thing: "He read that slip yesterday. Read it twice, same as the boy did. Put it back under the stone and took the fence. I'd have done the same, and I told him, and he said he knew."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-08.md:189:"The reason," Lira said, "is the number. It's a hundred because it's been under a stone a fortnight and the man who's nine lines up read it twice and put it back." She had a hand flat on the table, on the taped side, the way she put a hand down when she meant to stand and was not standing yet. "Two days past Thornwater. We've walked worse for forty. You've got three ribs and a calf; say that before he does." A nod, small, at the fire, where Pike was. "I'm saying it's a walk and a carry. I'm not saying it isn't the other thing."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-20.md:21:They went down the forty steps with two lamps.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-20.md:55:"Five years," Seln said. He had come to it as he came to everything, along the wall, reading the ground, and he stood over the open book with his hands at his sides. "By the scale on the case. By the stakes. He was working. He stopped in the middle of a word and put the pen down and left the book open and the cord out and the case's lid on the floor, and he went up forty steps, and he didn't come back for any of it." He did not kneel. "Nobody leaves a registry kit. It's signed out. You don't get another."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-20.md:99:"Forty-one," she said.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-20.md:103:"Forty-one kinds of line. The Ring has forty-one. I counted them for four days and wouldn't say the number, because a count from one place is a count. The Stair has forty-one." She picked up the tally and held it so he could see the strokes, which he could not read and did not need to. "Every kind that's on those eleven uprights is on these four walls. Every one. Not one kind here that isn't there, and not one there that isn't here — except one."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-20.md:111:"The floor was carved by somebody who knew the circle's size," Karis said. "That's the finding. I'm entering it as that, and I'm entering it as nothing else." She stood up. "A measurement is a fact about a ruler, not about the hand that held it. I've a measurement. I've got the same number three times now — the cord's, the ledger's, the wall's — and three's a fact, and the fact is *two hundred meters*, and the fact stops there. Whose ruler, and why, and what the other forty-one are saying — I don't know, and I'm not going to know from a wall, and I'm not going to write a guess in the small hand over a true unknown and let the record carry it forward." She began, without hurry, to pick up the sheets in the order she had laid them down. "Twenty-nine sheets. Two sites. One line that measures the circle from the inside. Entered."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-20.md:125:He took it by the strap with his good hand and put it on his good shoulder, and Lira said, "I've got a hand," and Brom said, "No," and Oryn said, "That's a mended shoulder," and Brom said, "It's the other one," and picked up the middle box as well, by its strap, in the same hand, so that he went up forty steps of fused stone one man wide with a survey kit and a field archive on his left side and his right side carried still and forward, a man carrying a thing he has been told not to use, and refused help twice more on the way, once from Cael at the twentieth step and once from Lira at the top, who had a hand out again and had it looked at.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-20.md:133:*Hundred and fifteenth day. The Long Stair, the rim, at the stone. A hundred and two in hand; nothing owed; three hundred entered as taken and never to be paid. Nineteen. Ten. Brom's shoulder mended and not used. Lira's arm closed and not used. Seln's got a ledger. Karis has twenty-nine sheets and a number, and the number is forty-one.*
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-24.md:57:Pike had one slip that had waited since the autumn — the axe-head hold's long fence, forty marks, HOLD, Teague, which Teague's crew had walked in the autumn and had not finished because the Stair had taken them south — and Pike said, to the book, "Fence. Forty. Teague says it's yours if you want it; he's got the winter road," and Brom, who priced fences, said, "Ours," and Pike wrote HOLD and the five names under it, and the winter had a job in it, which was the thing a winter needed. Karis took the inn's back room for the sheets, at a price the woman who kept the inn named and Karis paid without counting, which Cael had never once seen her do with money. Seln bought nothing, and sat at the door end, and watched the landing, and when the ferryman came in to say the sack was closing at noon, Seln looked at the sack and at his own coat where the ledger had been and was not, and said nothing, because he had given the ledger to Karis on the road and had not asked for it back and was not going to.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-24.md:61:Hesk's satchel. He had carried it out of a residence room on a bluff a year ago with the log in the pocket built for it, and it had been on every road since, waxed leather over a frame built for forty years of weather, the spare buckle still sewn flat on the strap's underside where it would not chafe, and the leather had gone dark along the strap where his shoulder took it and the brass had gone dull at every corner and not one seam had opened, because a man who had watched a strap fail on a road had built it so that it would not fail on anyone he liked. He set it on his knee by the fire, and opened the flap, and took the log out, and the four of them let him, because the inventory was a thing they had watched him write at the end of every year since Greyvane and had learned to sit still for.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-24.md:83:A straightedge. Brass, folding, three sections on two pins so that it lay in the hand the length of a palm and opened to the length of a forearm, and the edge true — he sighted along it against the fire and there was no light under the line — and calibrated, in the small struck marks he had known before he could read, along its whole length, in a hand that had cut the marks on every gauge in a shop in Denvash for forty years. Instrument-maker's work. Six weeks late.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-18.md:121:"Line," she said, and it was not a question. Lira went with her, one-armed, on the taped foot, along the shoulder, a stride in from where Karis walked; and at forty paces Lira said, "Gone," in the voice she used for a distance, and stepped back, and said, "Back," and Karis put a stone down where she stood, a fist of ridge-rock, in the road's cut, and looked at it, and looked at the bowl, and did not say the word she had coined at a table, because she did not need to; the stone said it.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-21.md:63:Karis counted. He held it. Forty. Sixty. Every stone on the floor had gone quiet in fourteen; this did not go quiet at all, and the thing under the spring went back and forth across one fixed place as a dog goes back and forth under a bone it cannot reach.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-19.md:9:Forty meters in, Brom stopped as a man stops who has walked into a door.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-19.md:17:A bowl of dead Path, forty meters deep from the line they had just crossed. A rim round it at the height of a wall, where Paths worked. Six figures on the rim — he counted them as he had counted hounds, without seeing one clearly: two on the south lip above the road's far cut, two on the east above the scrub, one west, one on the north rim above the stone they had crossed, all of them in road grey with hoods up, all of them with the long shape of a crossbow held the same way, all of them still. They had let six people walk in and six Paths die at the stride, and waited until the six were forty meters from any line, and put one bolt into the largest of them from a hundred and twenty meters of ground where their own Paths ran, and had not needed to be careful, because on this floor nobody could answer.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-19.md:33:Not for the line. She had heard *closed*, and she had heard *rim*, and she had been the fastest thing in every room she had ever stood in, and she did the arithmetic in the length of a stride and ran *down* — off the road's cut, across the scrub, toward the floor, toward the one thing in the bowl that had a hole in it. The stair. Forty meters of open ground with six crossbows above it, one-armed, on a taped foot, with no burst in her and the shape of one going through her back anyway, and a bolt skipped off the fused stone a yard ahead of her and went singing off into the scrub, and she did not slow.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-19.md:45:The stair was forty steps.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-19.md:51:He catalogued it by lantern because it was what he did. Square, or near it. Ten strides by ten. Fused stone, floor and walls, level, seamless. The ceiling a hand above Brom's head, and it was the floor — the underside of the plate they had run across, one piece of grey, no join, so that six people stood under the Long Stair's floor as under a table. The walls carved floor to ceiling in the lines, in bands, regular as a rule, the same kind of line and never the same line, and Karis, with a bolt-wound man beside her and six crossbows forty steps up, looked at the walls for the length of one breath before she looked at anything else, and he saw her see them, and he saw her put it away. At the chamber's middle, shapes: something cased, something laid out on the stone in a line, a cord. Nobody went to look. There was no time to look, and it was not what they had come down for.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-19.md:55:A stair's width. A man's height. Fused stone either side. A doorway five people with no Paths could hold against anyone at all, because to come down the stair a man had to walk into the Quiet at the line and then forty steps down a flight one man wide into a lantern, with no Path of his own by then either, and at the bottom of it a big man with a bill-hook's haft and a bolt in him, a girl with a knife, and a fifteen-year professional with another one.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-19.md:121:He went up the forty steps with the ten held to nothing but one, and the one was Seln's.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-19.md:127:Dusk. The light going off the rim and the rim's figures gone to shapes against a sky the colour of the floor. Six. He counted them again, from the hole, because a count is a count: two south, two east, one west, one north above the stone, all still, all the same shape, all of them scanning a bowl for the one thing they were sure could not happen in it. A hundred and twenty meters of scrub and fused stone between him and the stone in the road's cut, and the rim's door shut along the line either side of it, which he had seen from forty meters in that morning and did not need to see again to know where it was.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-19.md:131:Not fast. Fast was a shape. He walked as Seln walked, as a man walks who is not there — the presence thinned to nothing, every step folded into the dusk, the floor and the lip and the scrub taken on his own feet with nothing under his soles but the ground, and no sound in any of it. Forty meters of floor. The lip. Sixty meters of scrub, knee-high, grey in the failing light, with six crossbows above it that were looking for a shout and did not have one to look at. Nobody on the rim moved. He was the loudest thing in the bowl and it was a loudness whose whole grammar was silence, and the rim did not have a form for it, because the rim had been told that inside the line there was nothing to hear and had believed it, and was, on that one point, exactly right about every practitioner in Valdris but one.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-23.md:79:Lira went with her, one-armed, on the taped foot, down the ridge's near slope, a stride in from where Karis walked, and at forty paces Lira did not say *gone*. At sixty she did not. At a hundred, on a slope where at the Ring and the Stair and the Hall she had gone dark at forty, she was still walking with her hand a little out from her side and her Arbiter with her, and she stopped, and looked back up the slope at the others, and said nothing, and went on.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-23.md:101:Nobody said anything. Cael counted the stakes he could see, because a count was what he had, and stopped at forty because the dusk stopped him, and they had not stopped.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-23.md:115:Seln said, "There's forty stakes I can see and there's more I can't. They've been here more than once. A cordon's a thing you put round a place you mean to come back to." He did not say *and they know which crew walks out of quiet ground*, because he had said it at a plank and once was the number.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-23.md:133:They camped on the crest with the fire small and the cordon below them in the dark, forty stakes and more, and the shape four hundred meters in past the iron, in a quiet that none of them had a word for and none of them was going to make one, and in the morning they would turn for Lowmarch, five, with a kit and a ledger and fifty-two sheets of copied walls and a straightedge and a map with four circles on it and a line through all four.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-16.md:155:Vastin received the broker's completion report as a courtesy copy, forwarded through a registry office that had no interest in any board east of the Line, in the second folder of the morning, marked *Fallow Ring: crew returned, instruments not recovered*. He read it twice — a broker's report from a town on no registry map, on his desk with no routing note — and did not know why it had reached him, and filed that, in the register of a man who had never once in forty years pretended to knowledge he lacked, as the whole of what he knew about it. He returned the copy to its folder unannotated.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-17.md:27:"I've kept this wall thirty years. Thirteen crews on it now, and I've had forty-odd across the years, and I've walked past the Fallow Ring two hundred times and never once stepped over its line, and I've never once had a crew on my sheet that did. Not once. Not twice, which is the number the road counts." He looked at the eighth line where it lay. "You went in. You came out. Six in and six out, and the sixth a healer, and the only thing in my book that's got room for that is the outcome column, and the outcome column says *incomplete*." He picked the pen up. "The wall's the wall. I've moved you down. I'm telling you what the wall can't say, because I keep it and I'd sooner you heard it from the man who keeps it than worked it out from the room."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-17.md:55:Three hundred. The Fallow one had been a hundred, dry, and had been the best number on the board by forty. This was three times that, for a site one day south instead of four days east, and it hung on a nail where every crew on the wall had read it and put it back.
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-17.md:95:"Walk away." She said it to the table, flat, the arm across her chest and the taped foot up on the bench where the two days had put it. "That's the honest one. It's a trap. Seln's just told us it's a trap, and he's told us what it's for, and it's for him and it's for you, and I've got one arm. We don't take it. We take the axe-head fence at forty and the autumn walk at fifty and we pace the carter's third circle on our own time when nobody's paid us to walk into it. There's no rule that says the site has to be on somebody else's slip."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-17.md:97:"Yes," Brom said. It was the weight being named. "What she said. We've a hundred and eight and a mule. We don't need three hundred. We need the winter, and the winter's on the wall at forty and fifty and fed." He looked at the slip, under its stone, across the room. "Three hundred's a number a man doesn't reach for. My father said that about a horse once. He was right about the horse."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-17.md:101:"I don't care who posted it. I care that it's a site." She had the carter's map out already, the second blank under her thumb. "It's on the bearing from the Ring — near enough; I'll know when I've paced it. It's a day off. It's got stakes in it, because he's stamped them, which means it's been *measured*, which means when I measure it I'll have a second number to put against my first, and one number is a count and two is a fact. I would go alone. I'm saying so plainly so nobody has to guess. If the five of you take the fence, I take the map and a cord and a crossbow I don't know how to wind and go and pace the Long Stair by myself, and you can enter that in the ledger under whatever column you like."
books/book-07-void-roads/chapters/chapter-17.md:103:"We know," Lira said. "That's why we're at this table and not already at the fence."

