   185	He counted strides, since somebody would want the number. And he did what a one-armed man had told them to do, though it had not been said to him. He turned his attention inward, to the place behind his breastbone where a small bright thing had gathered on the morning of his Kindling. He had gone looking for it a few times since, in the first months, on bad nights. He looked now. It was as it had always been: a dark place, like a niche in a wall where a lamp once stood lit.
   186	
   187	*Seventy-eight. Seventy-nine.*
   188	
   189	At the eightieth stride Lira's hand went up.
   190	
   191	She had stopped dead, on both feet, as she had taught herself to stop on a gravel bar. Her head was a little on one side.
   192	
   193	"It's like the air got thick."
   194	
   195	Brom had halted in the same instant. He was frowning at the grass in front of his boots, and his big hands had come a little away from his sides.
   196	
   197	"Like a door closing behind me."
   198	
   199	Cael looked along the row. The healer had not spoken at all. Both her hands had risen, half way, with the palms turned up and the fingers spread, as a person holds them out to learn whether it has begun to rain; and she was staring at them.
   200	
   201	"My Arbiter's gone."
   202	
   203	That was Karis. Her voice was quite level, and her face had no colour in it whatever.
   204	
   205	"Back up. Ten paces. Tell me when it comes back."
   206	
   207	Seln had not raised his voice. He stood at the north end of the row with his hands at his sides, for once, and Cael had never before heard him give an order as though he expected to be obeyed.
   208	
   209	They stepped back.
   210	
   211	"Back," said Lira; and Brom said it with her, and Karis, and the healer, and Seln himself, a breath behind the rest. It was five voices on the one word. Not one of them had taken a second step.
   212	
   213	"Same stride. All of us."
   214	
   215	Karis was looking down the row, from face to face. Then she went forward again by herself, a foot at a time, with her hand held out in front of her as though she expected to touch something; and at a certain place she stopped, and drew back her toe by the width of a hand, and put it out again. She bent, and felt about in the roots of the grass, and came up with a grey stone the size of a loaf.
   216	
   217	She set it down on the place.
   218	
   219	Cael had gone forward the eighty strides with them, and back the one. He stood now on the near side of a stone, with five people who had each lost a thing and had it given back, and nothing in him had altered at either step. The dark place was dark. He had looked at it going over and looked at it coming back, and he could not have told the two apart.
   220	
   221	The nine fragments lay in him where they had lain since a ford. He was sure of that as well. When Lira's hand went up, Wind had leaned in his own feet to go with hers, as it had leaned every day for two years, and he had put it down without thinking; and that had been on the far side of where the stone now stood. Anchor was asking about the stone already. *There?*
   222	
   223	He did not know what it meant.
   224	
   225	And nobody had turned to him. He saw why, and it was the simplest thing in the world. All four of them knew what he was. They had known for years that a brass plate had waited eleven seconds over him and gone dark, and that nothing had spoken to him out of that dark since. A line that put out a lamp had nothing in him to put out. It had not occurred to one of them to ask whether he had felt it, any more than they would have asked a man without a purse whether he had been robbed. The fifth knew nothing of brass plates, and she was looking at her own two hands.
   226	
   227	So there were two reasons, and he looked at each of them squarely, standing in the long light. He did not know what he would be telling them. And he had not been asked.
   228	
   229	Neither one was a good enough reason by itself. He knew that. He kept silent on the pair of them together, and his own shadow lay out in front of him across a grey stone and on into the grass.
   230	
   231	---
   232	
   233	He wrote it at the stone, kneeling, while there was still light enough to see the page.
   234	
   235	*Ninety-fifth day. The Fallow road, at the rise. Eighty strides east of where the ruts turn.*
   236	
   237	*Quiet ground. Karis's Arbiter stopped at a line she can mark with a rock. So did Lira's, Brom's, Seln's, Oryn's — same line, same step. Mine didn't. Mine's been stopped since I was fourteen. I don't know what that means. Nobody asked. Tomorrow we go in.*
   238	
   239	*The nine: held. Deployment since the Line: one.*
   240	
   241	He read it over. It was short, and it was set down as he would have set down a count, and under it he could feel the size of what he had not put.
   242	
   243	They made their camp at the turn of the ruts, eighty yards back from the stone, and there was no fire. Seln had chosen the place. It lay a little below the crest on the western side, so that a person lying down could not see the ring and could not be seen from it. They ate cold bread from Thornwater's oven, and the hold's blankets went round, and the mule stood with her tail to the wind.
   244	
   245	"Two and two," said Seln, "and nobody alone. I shall take the first with whoever will have it."
   246	
   247	"I will," said Brom.
   248	
   249	The mule had settled the matter of the line for herself an hour before. She had been left with her rein under a rock, and she had drawn it out and grazed her way down the old road; and when Oryn went to fetch her she was standing with her forefeet on one side of the grey stone and her hind feet on the other, pulling at the grass, as easy as a beast in its own paddock.
   250	
   251	"She doesn't know it's there," said Lira.
   252	
   253	"She's nothing in her for it to take." Oryn had the rein, and she stood looking at her animal's four small hooves with an expression that Cael could not read. "I've walked her round this place thirty times, by the ruts, because I was told to. I never once thought to ask what she'd have done if I'd let her choose."
   254	
   255	Shadow lifted then, as it lifted at every dusk. Cael felt it come up soft behind the other eight, as it had come up beside a turf fire on the fifth day and at a ford on the fifty-third, offering to take him out of the light; and he told it no. It had come at the turn of the ruts as it came anywhere. He wondered whether it would have come a stride beyond the stone, and thought that he knew, and did not go to see.
   256	
   257	He walked up to the crest once more before he lay down, since he wished to see the place with the day going off it.
   258	
   259	The sun was under the western hills. The grass had gone grey, and the eleven stones were black against the last of the sky, and all the long slope between had lost its colour and lay like ash. Only one thing in that whole country was holding the light. It was the pale level thing among the feet of the stones. He still could not have said what it was, at three hundred yards. It lay there like a pool, and the last light stood on it as light stands on water after the banks have gone dark; and it went on shining, faintly, for a long while after it should have stopped.
   260	
   261	He was not the only one who had come up to look.
   262	
   263	The healer was at the stone. She stood on the near side of it, with her boots together, like a person at the edge of a pond. She had both arms stretched out in front of her at the height of her waist, so that her two hands were over the line and the rest of her was not, and the palms were turned down.
   264	
   265	She kept them so. He did not go nearer, and he did not speak. He watched a square dark figure stand at a stone with its hands held out over nothing, as still as the uprights beyond, until the pale thing on the far slope had faded and there was no light left anywhere for her to see them by.

