   301	One had come out of the mild man who stood six feet off with his hands behind him, looking east. It was the very thing that Seln had reached for at this stone, not half an hour ago, and not found; and Cael had held it shut at every dusk for ninety-six days, so that nobody who might one day speak of it to a carter bound for a ferry should know where a boy had learned it. A healer spoke to every carter on seven roads. She would not have meant the least harm by it.
   302	
   303	That was not his alone to give away. It was a real reason, and he distrusted it for coming so readily to hand.
   304	
   305	"It was dark before," said Cael. "It's dark now."
   306	
   307	Both halves were true. He had looked at that place going over and coming back, and it had been the same dark. Karis wrote. He watched her pencil put the two halves down, one after the other, and he knew what she would take them to mean, and what the other four would take them to mean when they read her leaf; and that the whole was not the truth, though there was not a false word in it.
   308	
   309	It was the first thing of that kind that he had kept from any of them in a long while, and he had done it at a stone, at noon, with a pencil going.
   310	
   311	"The sun's over," said Lira. She was looking up, with her hand shading her eyes. "If we're going, it's now."

