editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:211:A stillhound hunted what went out of a person. Every hold in that country kept its Paths in its pockets on a road because of it; and here was a place, a quarter of a mile across, where a Path could not be taken out of a pocket by anybody. A deer that wandered over that line was a deer. A carter who did was a man with a stick. And five people who had cleared a ford with a declaration and a knife were standing at the door of the den with a hedging bill and two knives, and a healer whose hands had stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:239:*The circle is perfect. The stakes are the registry's. The dogs are inside. Everyone's Path dies at the line and mine didn't, and I've told nobody, and I'm going to need to think about why I haven't. Not tonight. Tonight we're inside the one place on the continent where a stillhound can't lose.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:5:She did not go alone, since Seln had made a rule and she had lost no time in seeing the sense of it. Lira had the watch with her. Lira sat down in the grass ten yards short of the place, with her back turned and her knife across her knees, to look at everything that was not a ring of stones; and Karis went on the last ten yards by herself, with a notebook, and a pencil, and no lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:23:She counted them, and at each crossing she made herself attend to one thing only, as she would have made a student attend. The third time she watched for the instant, and it had no width that she could find. The fifth time she went over slowly, a hand's breadth at a time, to learn whether it dimmed; it did not, and then it was gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:45:It was very tidy, and she made herself look at that, because it was what she was afraid of.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:137:That made three. He could rule the line now, and it would be straight. What he could not do was say which way along it he was looking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:211:"A circle is a made shape."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:103:There was the grass, and the short noon shadows, and a stride beyond the stone a patch of bent stems where two boots had been. Lira made a small sound. She had the best eyes of the five, and Cael saw them go the whole length of the slope and come back with nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:211:"Is it Brom?" said Karis. "Forgive me; I must ask it. His Arbiter stopped at that stone, and yours did. If the thing that you read is his current, and his current is shaped by his Path, then perhaps there was nothing in him to read."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:215:Brom stood on the western side of the stone, where he was an Iron Skin with his Path in the next room. He stretched out his left arm. Oryn kept her two hands on it, and she stepped over the stone by herself, so that she stood inside and he outside, with the mended wrist held between them across the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:261:He had not meant to say it aloud. It had been going round in him since the plank, a thing that he could not sort. A man had walked a road for thirty years, and each time that he came to a certain place he had gone round, and each time he had made a mark in his head. He had never once put his foot over, and he knew the number of his own refusals to the unit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:271:It was true, and he had not known it, and she had. He had refused nine things a stride at a time for ninety days, and thought himself a careful person; but a refusal that is made again at every stride is the opposite of a rule, for it is a door that somebody is holding shut with his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:19:She said it quite quietly. Two lines that had stood over theirs since the day they came now stood under them, and the shingle pair were at the foot still. Cael looked for what the rung was made of, since he had learned in this room that it was always made of something. It was a mountain, less a cart. The wall had no place to say so. It said only that a hold which asked Pike who ought to walk a bad hill would now be given five names before it was given two others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:113:Cael had set the cream sheet in the middle of the table, where all five could reach it, and he gave the order as he would have given it for any contract: each of them to speak, and nobody to answer until the last had done. Brom poured. He had allowed one jug, on the strength of a line said at a plank, and had made it plain that the paper under the lamp had not yet earned a second.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:147:He had a good many reasons. He could have said what he had said to Lira under the eave, that he wished to see it, and it would have been true. He could have said that a hold had asked for them by name, and that a crew which was asked for ought to find out what it was made of. There was a rope with no figure against it. There was a cart in a gorge, and a line in a book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:229:Teague was there. He stood a little way out from the eave, on the trodden grass, with his thumbs in his belt, and his three behind him at their distances: the spear, grounded, and the bow on the good leg, and the boy with a heel of bread. That made four. The Oxhollow carter had come up from his mules and stood chewing, which was five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:39:They spent the rest of that day getting ready for a road. The Oxhollow carter had his off mule at the smith's for a shoe. Brom went over the three box-ropes a hand's breadth at a time, since they were all the rope that the five now owned. Seln was gone from the meadow between the mail and dusk, and came back with pale dust on his boots, and said nothing; and Cael, who had made a promise at that plank, looked at the dust and then at the fire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:45:It lay as it had lain on four other crossings, dry and red, thirty yards from lip to lip, with a ramp cut down either side and the thorn stump standing on its eastern edge. The carter took his mules through at a walk and halted them on the far flat to blow, and the five sat down among the chock-stones where they had twice made a camp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:247:Karis got down, and crossed the yard with her hands held a little away from her sides, as she had carried them when they were bound, and stood before the lamp. Cael saw her draw a breath. Then her right hand made the smallest dip that he had ever seen it make, as a person sets a full stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:53:The five waded after. It was a hundred yards of gravel under a foot of quick water, cold enough to make the bones of his feet ache. The tape told him where the edge was whenever a stone turned under him. He kept his breath shallow and his eyes on the cart's tail. Seln waded a little upstream of him. He had taken up the strap of the locked case by two holes before he stepped in, so that it rode high between his shoulder-blades, well clear of the river; it had been on him on every road since their own cart was sold, and he had declined, mildly, to let it ride on another man's bed with the boxes. Three boxes sat on it under a sheet of patched canvas, left and middle and right, with the corner that was worn white turned outward. On top of them, roped down by her own instructions, sat Karis, who had been lifted up there like a sack and had made no objection.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:59:The first prints were a man's, of the middle size, set down evenly. Beside the right foot, here and there, was a small round dent, where the haft of a hand-axe had swung at his thigh and touched ground on the slope. The second walker had grounded a spear at every step. The third set of marks was shorter on the left side than on the right by the width of two fingers, every stride, and no deeper for it; whoever made them had been favouring that hip for so long that the body no longer argued. The fourth were the smallest feet of the four and went in the deepest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:69:He had not expected to enjoy it. But there was a pleasure in reading a thing well made, and he had it now, as he had had it from the east tier of a yard, watching a good pair work. The difference was that he did not want to beat this one. He had no notion what he wanted from it; the man had told him to be good enough to be glad of, and Cael was reading the silt to learn what *good enough* walked like.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:93:Brom made a sound into his sleeve. Lira looked at the boots, and at the road going away south between its alders, six days of it, and at her own bare foot with the linen crossed over it in a figure of eight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:163:Cael had half risen to say something, and he sat down again. He had been on the point of telling Brom that the week was out, and that he was free of it. He saw now that it would have been like telling a man the price of something he had made himself. Nobody had kept that week but Brom. He could finish it in whatever way he pleased.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:169:The valley had flattened and gone wet. For two hundred yards and more in front of them there was no pasture and no alder, only black water standing in sheets among yellow reed, with tussocks between as big as kneeling sheep; and across the whole of it somebody had laid a road. It was made of logs. They lay side by side, crosswise, each as thick as a man's leg and twice as long as the cart was wide, bedded on brushwood and sunk to half their depth. They ran away over the moss in a grey ribbed strip, like the backbone of something very long.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:187:It was the fan again, Cael thought, and he was glad to see that it was the fan made small; there was no slope in it and nothing overhead. But somebody had to learn which logs were sound before a wheel was trusted to any of them, and there was only one way that the five of them had ever done that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:209:His left hand had been in a sling and a cuff, by turns, since a river on the fifty-third day. That made thirty-five days. Cael watched the two wrists, as he had watched them under a cart on the eighth narrowing, and no grey started at either. There was a hundredweight of copied paper on them and they were the colour of wrists.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:225:They made their last camp on the evening of the eighty-ninth day, on a halting-ground at the top of a long hill, with a river shining below them in the dusk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:229:Karis was awake. She had been awake since noon, sitting up between the boxes with her notebook on her knee, and she had the colour back in her face that six days of sleep could give it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:261:*Mine: I breathe to the tape and walk to a mule. Brom: the week kept, to the hour, and three boxes carried over a moss. Lira walks. Karis is awake.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:3:Karis made her long cord along Thornwater's fence in the first grey light, with Brom to do the knots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:243:They made their camp at the turn of the ruts, eighty yards back from the stone, and there was no fire. Seln had chosen the place. It lay a little below the crest on the western side, so that a person lying down could not see the ring and could not be seen from it. They ate cold bread from Thornwater's oven, and the hold's blankets went round, and the mule stood with her tail to the wind.

