editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:243:For a quarter of an hour after that, nobody at the stone had an eye to spare for anybody else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:67:Cael came and looked. All along the brink, as far as he could follow it to either hand, the floor turned up and rolled over on itself, smooth, with no corner anywhere, so that its crest stood a finger above the grey within and a hand above the turf outside. He had stepped over that a quarter of an hour ago and never marked it. A person running would have had to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:125:Cael had expected a good many things of this place, and that had not been among them. He stood on a grey floor with the paper's second sentence going through his head: instruments, and abandoned, and a site. Somebody had sat at a ruled desk and written that, with the sum in words and again in a bracket, and had sent it over a river with a hundred marks in a bag. It had lain a month under a stone. A captain had read it twice and put it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:141:"Two stones more," said Karis. She had seen what he was doing. "The tenth and the eleventh. A quarter of an hour, if Lira doesn't sneeze. I shall never stand here again; you heard what he said about going in twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:143:He weighed it. He had set a limit, and he knew what a limit was worth that gave by a quarter of an hour the first time it was leaned on. He also knew that nine sheets out of eleven was a thing she would carry for the rest of her life. And the hillside was as empty as it had been at noon. No bird had got up. Nothing had stirred the grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:149:"I did. I'm spending a quarter of an hour that I told you I wouldn't, and I want it entered against me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:165:Karis finished the eleventh sheet with the sun a hand lower than he had measured it. The tenth had torn under her pencil, and she had done it over from the first stroke; her quarter of an hour had run to three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:219:It was on the ridge. He had measured two hands under it, and given away a quarter of an hour, and let the quarter run to three; and the lower rim of it was touching the hills now, red and flattened, and going. The shadows of the eleven stones had run out across the floor and joined. Between them, where the last light struck it level, the grey had begun to shine.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:149:It took the better part of a quarter of an hour. The shadow came in to the width of three fingers and stood there, and stood, and then, by a hair that Cael would not have sworn to, it was on the other side of the rod.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:43:"Teague's nine up," Pike said, to the book, asked by nobody. Then, still to the book, at the same size, as if it were the next line and not a different thing: "He read that slip yesterday. Read it twice, same as the boy did. Put it back under the stone and took the fence. I'd have done the same, and I told him, and he said he knew."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:131:"A survey kit's a cart's worth. Brass. A level, a chain, the rule. Nobody puts a cart's worth down and walks off from it unless they walked off fast." He looked at the jug. "That's not the reason. That's what it is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:225:Pike took it without a word. He read it twice, though he could have said it in his sleep, and then he turned his shoulder to the meadow and gave it out: the recovery, and the Fallow road, and the rise, and the hundred. It was the first time in a month that those words had been heard on that grass. He said the whole of it a second time. Then he said *taken*, and five names, and dipped his pen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:25:"I had the last one read in a quarter of an hour, three times over, and then there was nothing left of it." He turned the red-ruled square in his fingers. "I've had one letter in ninety days. Now I've two, and four days of road in front of me. I'm going to have one of them at the wash tomorrow, at the noon halt. I'll keep the other for a noon with nothing in it."

